      "tip": "In Bangladesh you rarely call an adult by their name or 'sir/madam'. Instead you **promote them into your family**:\n\n- **ভাই / ভাইয়া** — any man around your age or a bit older (shopkeepers, waiters, colleagues)\n- **আপা / আপু** — any woman a bit older than you\n- **চাচা** — an older man; **খালা** — an older woman (also domestic helpers)\n- **মামা** — rickshaw drivers, tea-stall owners, street vendors: friendly and informal\n- **আংকেল / আন্টি** — friends' parents and older people in middle-class city life\n\nThese terms still take **আপনি** (polite verbs) unless you know the person well: *Chacha, apni kemon achhen?*\n\nUsing them makes people warm to you instantly — a foreigner who says **মামা** to a rickshaw driver will usually get a big smile.",
